    "https://clarku.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203043": {
      "title": "Research Grant Portfolio and Operations Manager",
      "description": "Clark University\nJob Posting: Research Grant Portfolio and Operations Manager Clark University \n\tJob Posting: Research Grant Portfolio and Operations Manager Title Home Human Resources Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Research Grant Portfolio and Operations Manager Category: Staff and Administrators Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: PROV-Provost Office Expenses 2111 Locations: Worcester, MA Posted: Jul 27, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time - Exempt Salary Type: Annually Salary Range: $60,900.00 - $78,650.00 Position ID: 203043 Share About Clark University: Founded in 1887, Clark was one of the first all-graduate institutions in the United States. Today the University is a highly-ranked, student-centered institution educating approximately 2,350 undergraduate and 1,150 graduate students to be imaginative and contributing citizens of the world and to advance the frontiers of knowledge and understanding through rigorous scholarship and creative effort. This commitment to scholarship and inquiry reflects the University's commitment to \"challenge convention and change our world\" and to address issues of critical importance to society. It is also reflected by Clark's many national and international distinctions, including innovation, community engagement and impact, as a top green campus, and for having a distinguished geography and international development program. Clark has also been included in the groundbreaking Colleges that Change Lives guide since it was first published in 1996. Clark is located in Worcester, Massachusetts, a dynamic, diverse city \"on the rise\". The second largest city in New England, Worcester is home to 11 institutions of higher learning and is increasingly recognized for its growing healthcare and biotechnology communities, its thriving cultural scene, and as a vibrant food hub. Job Description: Essential Duties and Responsibilities Pre-Award Grant Proposal Preparation Coordinate with faculty and research scientists to prepare budgets and financial sections of grant proposals to federal agencies, foundations, and other funders (approximately 30 proposals annually). Review proposal documents for completeness and compliance, assemble full proposal packages in Cayuse for submission through the Office of Sponsored Programs and Research (OSPR), and enter proposals into agency portals such as Research.gov, Grants.gov, and NSPIRES. Post-Award Grant Set-Up, Budgeting, and Compliance Coordinate execution of award documentation, contracts, and subcontracts; review award terms for compliance and reporting requirements with OSPR. Prepare grant set-up forms and account codes, coordinate payroll authorizations for student, postdoctoral, and staff researchers, monitor and reconcile grant budgets, and advise PIs on sponsor budget requirements. Institute Administrative Operations and Personnel Oversee day-to-day Institute operations in consultation with the Director and Steering Committee. Coordinate faculty and staff appointments, direct and evaluate staff overseen by the position, manage hiring processes, and communicate regularly with researchers and staff regarding administrative and personnel matters. Accounting Administration and Financial Reporting Prepare and track the Institute's operating budget in partnership with the Director, monitor indirect cost recovery, forecast revenues, prepare financial reports, and reconcile Banner accounts with Institute funds and supporting source documents. Institute and Endowed Fund Oversight Track spending and account balances for Marsh Institute endowed funds, including visiting lecturer reimbursements, the O'Connor Fund and HERO Summer Program paperwork, and Geller Award administration. Assist the Director with scheduling and convening Steering Committee meetings and identifying funding opportunities. Facilities and Information Systems Administration Oversee Institute facilities scheduling and use, coordinate purchases and installation of computer systems and equipment, liaise with Information Technology Services on access and technical issues, and maintain Institute databases, files, and mailing lists. Outreach, Communications, and Training Handle daily inquiries and correspondence, assist with the Marsh Institute Annual Report and website maintenance, coordinate lectures and seminars, attend relevant university and external meetings, and train and supervise support staff and work-study students on Institute systems and procedures. Requirements: Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in accounting or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience Minimum of 3-5 years of experience in research grant management, accounting administration, university administration, operations/project management, or a related field Ability to work independently with minimal supervision Proficiency with Microsoft Office products, including a high level of proficiency in Excel or similar spreadsheet/database software Familiarity with general accounting concepts and practices Demonstrated experience working with and contributing to a diverse workplace, with the ability to interact effectively with a broad range of constituents across campus Preferred Qualifications Experience with grant submission and management platforms such as Research.gov, Grants.gov, NSPIRES, or other agency proposal portals Experience using Banner Experience using Cayuse Prior experience supervising staff Knowledge of Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200) for federal grants management Additional Information: Expected Hiring Range:The posting salary range for the position is $60,900 to $78,650. Pay Transparency Disclosure: The compensation for this position will be determined based on factors that include available budget, internal equity, and the selected candidate's qualifications, experience, education, and other job-related credentials. This range represents Clark University's good-faith estimate of the expected hiring range at the time of posting consistent with Clark's compensation philosophy and internal alignment. At Clark University we believe that diversity of experiences, perspectives, and backgrounds leads to a more innovative and productive work environment. We welcome and encourage individuals of all backgrounds to join our team and contribute their unique ideas to help us achieve our goals. Clark University offers a generous benefit package for full and, if applicable, part-time employees that include; paid time off, generous retirement plan, group health and dental insurance, life insurance, and tuition, along with use of many campus amenities. For a complete list of benefits for eligible employees visit here . To review the Clark University Police Department Annual Security and Fire Safety Report visit here . Application Instructions: To be considered for this position, you must submit your credentials online. Create a Clark University Careers Account by clicking on the APPLY NOW button below. You will be able to upload the following documents, which are required for consideration: Resume Cover letter See the FAQ for using our online system. Please contact us if you need assistance applying through this website. Already have a Clark University Careers Account? Login to your account to add documents or update your account. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Salary will be commensurate with skills and experience. Applicants must be currently authorized to work in the United States for any employer. Clark University participates in E-Verify to confirm employment eligibility in the United States. A successful background check is required upon acceptance. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Clark University 950 Main Street Worcester, MA 01610"
